WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.697
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.247 --> 00:00:09.434
What advice do you have for
choosing recommenders?

00:00:11.762 --> 00:00:15.447
&gt;&gt; I don't know if applicants really
appreciate how important the letters of

00:00:15.447 --> 00:00:16.273
reference are.

00:00:16.273 --> 00:00:18.245
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; It's really the one opportunity that we

00:00:18.245 --> 00:00:21.455
get to see you through someone
else's eyes objectively.

00:00:21.455 --> 00:00:23.305
What did you do and how did you do it?

00:00:23.305 --> 00:00:27.120
So for me, put a lot of emphasis
on making the selection,

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.430
I can give you some tips
on how to think about it.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:34.190
At Stanford we request two, in one we
ask for a current direct supervisor, or

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:38.510
the best alternative,
if that is impossible or inappropriate.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:40.880
And for the second recommendation,
we also ask for

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.880
someone who's supervised your work,
but it could be from a nonprofit,

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:47.200
it could be extracurricular,
or from the same organization.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.850
And when you think about selecting them
what you really, just as I've asked

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.995
applicants in their essays to tell us
about the impact that they've had,

00:00:53.995 --> 00:00:56.920
because this past behavior
will predict future behavior,

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.440
that's the echo that we
want to see in a reference.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.286
So find someone who know's what you've
done, really been in the trenches and

00:01:03.286 --> 00:01:07.360
can see your work, and to describe not
just what happened but how you did it, and

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.990
how the way you did it might
have been different from others.

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.070
So that means don't worry
about the tittle of a person,

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:14.160
don't try to get the highest tittle.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.889
Also, I hear a lot of students asking,
or applicants asking,

00:01:16.889 --> 00:01:20.249
should I try to find someone who went
to Stanford because that's important?

00:01:20.249 --> 00:01:21.062
No it's not.

00:01:21.062 --> 00:01:27.620
It's actually what the recommender can
tell us about you that is most important.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:31.123
The second thing is,
after them understanding what you've done,

00:01:31.123 --> 00:01:31.989
is enthusiasm.

00:01:31.989 --> 00:01:32.494
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:01:32.494 --> 00:01:33.755
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] This is a hard, for

00:01:33.755 --> 00:01:36.710
someone who reads lots of
thousands of recommendations.

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:37.480
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:41.594
&gt;&gt; This one's a hard one to wrap your
hands around, but I know the best

00:01:41.594 --> 00:01:46.610
recommendations that I read are written
from both a head space and a heart space.

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:47.250
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; So

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.850
try to think about who you have
established a relationship with.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:52.990
Someone who really,
enthusiasm's a great word, but

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:56.120
has a connection with you, and
will be able to speak to that.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:59.430
And that's how recommendations
become most memorable.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:02.450
And then lastly, I think applicants
should think about the process.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:04.340
Don't ask for
a recommendation in an email.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:08.090
It's going to take this person
a significant amount of time.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:10.230
On average,
our references are about two pages.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:14.892
I'm guessing people have to spend at least
an hour or two to do a good job on them.

00:02:14.892 --> 00:02:19.070
What is most disappointing to me when I'm
really looking to find reasons to admit,

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:23.365
is I'll get to the end of an application,
I'll be on the second recommendation, and

00:02:23.365 --> 00:02:26.171
I'll see someone,
everything's been so positive and

00:02:26.171 --> 00:02:30.080
they'll write a positive recommendation,
but maybe only two paragraphs.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.180
And just say, they're phenomenal, they're
the best ever, they've done a great job,

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:37.290
but I won't have any of that
data to help me make a decision.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.298
And it just means that this particular
applicant doesn't have as much,

00:02:40.298 --> 00:02:44.040
I don't know, data, I guess is
the best word, but landscape for

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:47.200
me to really understand what they've
done and how they've done it.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.390
So take it seriously, make an appointment,

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:53.255
even if it's a 15 minuet chat, but
ask the question courageously, and say,

00:02:53.255 --> 00:02:57.225
will you write it, explain why, where
you're going, what schools, but then say,

00:02:57.225 --> 00:03:00.565
do you have time to invest
to do a good job on this?

00:03:00.565 --> 00:03:01.900
And if someone says they're really busy,

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:04.735
that's a good indication that maybe
you should go to your second choice.

00:03:04.735 --> 00:03:07.835
because it can make a difference in
the end of how much they're willing to

00:03:07.835 --> 00:03:10.230
spend time to help you on this process.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:13.615
&gt;&gt; And then for the recommenders,
are there any resources for them or

00:03:13.615 --> 00:03:17.194
how do they know how to write their
applicant the best letter they can?

00:03:17.194 --> 00:03:18.482
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] That's a great question.

00:03:18.482 --> 00:03:22.088
We try to give, and many recommenders
have written more than one before, but

00:03:22.088 --> 00:03:24.230
we try to give them clear advice and tips.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:28.292
When they're invited onto our portal
on the website, the questions will ask

00:03:28.292 --> 00:03:32.543
how has this person differentiated himself
from others that they've seen doing

00:03:32.543 --> 00:03:36.880
similar roles, and what have they done and
accomplished, so they'll get that.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:42.824
And, I think it comes down to that
they're willing to take the time to

00:03:42.824 --> 00:03:44.247
really support you.

00:03:44.247 --> 00:03:47.330
There's no magic there, but
they do have to make the investment and

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:50.970
be close enough to you to be able to write
thoughtfully about what you've done.

